    "title": "Declarative construction of test data",
    "body": "This is a tracking issue for individual refactorings of unit tests.\n\n**Rationale:** Primitive types like `CTxOut` currently provide read and write access to their data members like `scriptPubKey`. Consensus validation does not require write access. Hence, it is forced to depend on functionality that it does not use, which is a violation of the Interface Segregation Principle (ISP). A whole category of bugs could be eliminated if this functionality was removed.\n\n---\n\nOne case where write access to those data members is extensively used, is the **imperative building of test data** in unit tests like this:\n\nhttps://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/blob/9bf4815b94b9eaf8807f4e569091778da6c338d5/src/bench/ccoins_caching.cpp#L36-L52\n\nThe imperative building could be replaced with **declarative construction**:\n\n```cpp\nconst CTransaction tx_1{\n    std::array{\n        CTxIn{COutPoint{dummyTransactions[0].GetHash(), 1}, CScript{} << std::vector<unsigned char>(65, 0)},\n        CTxIn{COutPoint{dummyTransactions[1].GetHash(), 0}, CScript{} << std::vector<unsigned char>(65, 0) << std::vector<unsigned char>(33, 4)},\n        CTxIn{COutPoint{dummyTransactions[1].GetHash(), 1}, CScript{} << std::vector<unsigned char>(65, 0) << std::vector<unsigned char>(33, 4)},\n    },\n    std::array{\n        CTxOut{90 * COIN, CScript{} << OP_1},\n        CTxOut{},\n    },\n};\n```\n\nThis example shows several advantages:\n\n- It reduces the amount of necessary boilerplate.\n- It is necessarily honest: The fact that the outputs contain an empty element is plain to see rather than hidden in a `resize(2)` call.\n- It removes the need for a dedicated \"builder\" type, like `CMutableTransaction` in this case.\n\n---\n\nAnother case where write access is currently used, is the **reuse of test data**, like in this example:\n\nhttps://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/blob/9bf4815b94b9eaf8807f4e569091778da6c338d5/src/test/script_tests.cpp#L1287-L1339\n\nThe test data `txFrom` is constructed in line 1287 and later modified in lines 1315 and 1331, through the `scriptPubKey` reference defined in line 1289. Notably, each block of code that uses a different state of the test data is introduced by a comment containing the word \"case\" (\"Single signature case\", \"P2SH, single-signature case\", \"Hardest case:  Multisig 2-of-3\").\n\nSeparating the individual \"cases\" into *actually* independent test cases would remove the need for write access in addition to providing additional advantages:\n\n- Easier to reason about: The test input data is visible in a single place. It does not depend on modifications elsewhere or side effects of other tests.\n- Easier to parallelize: Individual test cases can run concurrently.\n\n---\n\nRefactoring the imperative code in unit tests is a precondition for solving the ISP issues of the primitive types. The two examples above show that such a refactoring is self-contained (it only affects test code, and in most cases only individual tests, so it can be done one test at a time). It also shows that it provides benefits for each individual test on its own.",
